<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jacopo Peri</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1561–1633</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jacopo Peri was a Florentine composer and singer associated with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florentine-camerata">Florentine Camerata</a> and the Medici court. His Dafne of about 1598, now largely lost, is regarded as the first opera, and his Euridice, performed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pitti-palace">Pitti Palace</a> in 1600 for the wedding of Maria de' Medici and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-iv-of-france">Henry IV of France</a>, is the earliest opera whose music survives. He developed the recitative style that underlay the new genre.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jacopo-peri.jpg" alt="Jacopo Peri" width="320" height="383" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bernardo Buontalenti (c. 1531 – 1608) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jacopo_Peri_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
